WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00.360 --> 00:07.661
Certainly, presenting remotely raises some interesting variations on the classic techniques we’ve been talking about.

2
00:07.661 --> 00:14.400
It’s not that anything you do in person that’s good becomes bad, but different things become more important.

3
00:14.730 --> 00:19.970
You still need to think about: where is my audience? Where do I want them to be?

4
00:20.420 --> 00:27.520
How do I connect with them emotionally? The empathy part of EPIC still holds true. And you still need to ask that question:

5
00:28.010 --> 00:34.980
what do I want them to think or feel or do as a result of this conversation we’re having? What is the purpose

6
00:35.430 --> 00:43.950
of meeting? And many of the techniques that work in a physical presentation will work in a virtual presentation, well — sometimes even better.

7
00:43.950 --> 00:45.990
The camera imposes new challenges,

8
00:46.660 --> 00:54.310
new opportunities. First of all, obviously, we’re seeing only a part of each person: we’re seeing their face mostly, and

9
00:54.760 --> 00:58.761
the top half of their body. So it’s different from walking around on a stage.

10
00:58.761 --> 01:06.400
Your body language, by contrast, needs to be a bit more restrained. It’s not going to help if you move around the place and waggle your arms a lot.

11
01:07.080 --> 01:14.750
And strangely, if you use your body language in person, this looks fine, but on a camera you can start to look like you have giant hands.

12
01:15.720 --> 01:21.760
So if you do move your hands, try to keep them a little bit more level, on the same plane as yourself physically.

13
01:22.120 --> 01:27.550
Think also about the level of your camera. You want your camera to be roughly at eye level.

14
01:28.290 --> 01:38.720
If it’s too high, you can start to look really small. And if it’s too low, people end up looking up your nose, and it looks like you think you’re terribly self-important.

15
01:39.490 --> 01:41.700
And so a nice balance is good for that.

16
01:43.010 --> 01:48.000
And finally, your eye contact works very strangely on camera. I’m sure you’ve worked this out already.

17
01:48.520 --> 01:56.930
But when you actually look at the faces, it looks like you’re not making eye contact. Now that’s okay, people are used to it.

18
01:57.670 --> 02:08.900
But if you have a really important key message to deliver, it’s worth looking direct at the camera and imagining the people behind that camera, because you will communicate more powerfully that way.

19
02:10.730 --> 02:18.910
Finally, a typical pitfall is looking at yourself. Switch yourself off, so that you can concentrate on the other people on the call,

20
02:19.270 --> 02:25.380
your audience — which is that third point of style, establishing the emotional connection.

